    {
      "id": "q-4",
      "type": "mcq",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "prompt": "Why is polynomial regression still called linear regression in its weights?",
      "options": [
        "Its plot must be straight",
        "The input powers are always one",
        "Its error is never squared",
        "Each learned weight multiplies a fixed feature power and the results are added"
      ],
      "answer": 3,
      "explanation": "Nonlinear input features can appear inside a model that remains linear in its coefficients."
    },
    {
      "id": "q-5",
      "type": "mcq",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "prompt": "Which pipeline setting avoids duplicating a ones column?",
      "options": [
        "PolynomialFeatures(include_bias=False) with an intercept-fitting regressor",
        "Set every coefficient to zero",
        "Use the target as the first feature",
        "Refit powers on test answers"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explanation": "LinearRegression already fits the intercept; a separate bias column is unnecessary."
    },
    {
      "id": "q-6",
      "type": "mcq",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "prompt": "Which split chooses the degree in an honest three-way workflow?",
      "options": [
        "Final test",
        "Validation",
        "Training score alone",
        "The smallest test residual row"
      ],
      "answer": 1,
      "explanation": "Use training to fit weights, validation to select settings, and final test to assess the chosen procedure."
    },
    {
      "id": "q-7",
      "type": "mcq",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "prompt": "Why can a degree-twelve curve behave badly beyond the observed range?",
      "options": [
        "The intercept disappears",
        "Extrapolation is guaranteed exact",
        "High powers magnify small input changes outside the fitted region",
        "Validation labels become training features automatically"
      ],
      "answer": 2,
      "explanation": "A flexible polynomial can grow rapidly away from the measured interval."
    },
    {
      "id": "q-8",
      "type": "output",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "prompt": "Which powers appear for one input x=2 at degree three without bias?",
      "options": [
        "[1, 2, 4]",
        "[2, 3, 4]",
        "[1, 2, 4, 8]",
        "[2, 4, 8]"
      ],
      "answer": 3,
      "explanation": "The columns are x,x squared,x cubed: 2,4,8.",
      "code": "from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\nprint(PolynomialFeatures(3,include_bias=False).fit_transform([[2]])[0].astype(int).tolist())\n",
      "requires": [
        "sklearn"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q-9",
      "type": "output",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "prompt": "Which degree wins validation losses 8,2,9 for degrees 1,3,12?",
      "options": [
        "3",
        "1",
        "12",
        "2"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explanation": "Degree 3 has the lowest validation loss, 2.",
      "code": "losses = {1: 8, 3: 2, 12: 9}\nprint(min(losses, key=losses.get))\n"
    },
    {
      "id": "q-10",
      "type": "output",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "prompt": "How many columns are in a one-feature degree-twelve design matrix including bias?",
      "options": [
        "12",
        "13",
        "11",
        "14"
      ],
      "answer": 1,
      "explanation": "Powers run from zero through twelve, giving thirteen columns.",
      "code": "degree = 12\npowers = list(range(degree+1))\nprint(len(powers))\n"
    }
  ],
  "keyPoints": [
    {
      "en": "Polynomial features add powers of the original input.",
      "bn": "polynomial features আসল ইনপুটের ঘাতের কলাম যোগ করে।"
    },
    {
      "en": "A polynomial stays linear in its learned weights.",
      "bn": "polynomial মডেল শেখা ওজনগুলোর দিক থেকে linear থাকে।"
    },
    {
      "en": "Lower training error does not guarantee better predictions.",
      "bn": "ট্রেনিংয়ের ভুল কমলেই নতুন ডেটায় ভালো হবে, এমন নয়।"
    },
    {
      "en": "Use validation to choose degree before opening the final test.",
      "bn": "শেষ টেস্ট দেখার আগে validation দিয়ে degree বেছে নাও।"
    },
    {
      "en": "High powers can cause numerical instability and poor extrapolation.",
      "bn": "বড় ঘাতে হিসাব অস্থির হতে পারে, বাইরের অনুমানও খারাপ হতে পারে।"
    }
  ],
  "examQuestions": [
    {
      "type": "numerical",
      "question": "Evaluate 2+x-2x²+x³ at x=0.5 and count its coefficients.",
      "marks": 5,
      "answerHtml": "<p>Powers are 0.5,0.25,0.125. Prediction=2+0.5-0.5+0.125=2.125. There are four coefficients including the intercept.</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "numerical",
      "question": "Candidates degree 1,3,12 have validation MSE 8,2,9. Choose a degree and explain final testing.",
      "marks": 5,
      "answerHtml": "<p>Degree three minimises validation MSE at 2. Fix that choice, optionally refit it on development data, then evaluate once on a separate final test. Training error alone is not the selection score.</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "long",
      "question": "Distinguish approximation, overfitting and numerical instability in polynomial regression.",
      "marks": 8,
      "answerHtml": "<p>A low-degree model can miss real curvature. A high-degree model can fit training noise and raise validation error. Ill-conditioned power columns can also make the numerical solve unstable. These are related but distinct issues requiring held-out evaluation and stable computation.</p>"
    }
  ],
